            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"538575\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\">In a DFA, from every state, for every input symbol, there must be exactly one transition. <br/> <br/> Here, from $q_0$ on input $1$, two transitions are possible.<br/> <br/> Hence, the automaton is not a DFA.</div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/538574/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-valid-dfa#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Finite Automata</a></b></div></div>"
