registerTest({
    "name": "DPP 13 - Theory of Computation (Regular Expression & Regular Languages - Part 1)",
    "testLabel": "DPP 13",
    "testType": "DPP",
    "bracket": "Regular Expression & Regular Languages - Part 1",
    "subject": "toc",
    "series": "cse-dpp-2027",
    "date": "Nov 03, 2026",
    "duration": "30 Min",
    "totalMarks": 11,
    "topicsCovered": "Regular Expression & Regular Languages (Practice Set 1)",
    "questions": [
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let, $\\Sigma=\\{a,b\\}$.</p><p>Consider the language, $L=\\{w\\in\\Sigma^*\\mid \\text{every occurrence of }a\\text{ is immediately followed by }b\\}$.</p><p>Which regular expression denotes $L$?</p><p>Here, $+$ denotes union.</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$(a^*b)^*$<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$(b+ab)^*$<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$a^*b$<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$b+(ab)^*$<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$(ab)^*$</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"556249\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>We need a regular expression that generates strings in which every $a$ is immediately followed by $b$.</p><p>Consider some strings belonging to $L$:</p><p>$\\epsilon,\\ b,\\ bb,\\ ab,\\ abb,\\ bab,\\ abab,\\ldots$</p><p>Strings such as $a$, $aa$, $aab$, and $aba$ do not belong to $L$.</p><p><br/>Every $a$ must appear as part of the block $ab$.</p><p>However, the symbol $b$ may also appear independently.</p><p>Therefore, every valid string can be formed by repeating either $b$ or $ab$.</p><p>This suggests $(b+ab)^*$.</p><p>Now,</p><p>Option B is $(b+ab)^*$</p><p>This expression allows any finite sequence of the blocks $b$ and $ab$.</p><p><strong>Examples:</strong></p><p>$$\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\\hline \\textbf{Blocks Chosen} &amp; \\textbf{Result}\\\\ \\hline \\text{None} &amp; \\epsilon\\\\ \\hline b &amp; b\\\\ \\hline ab &amp; ab\\\\ \\hline b,\\ ab &amp; bab\\\\ \\hline ab,\\ b &amp; abb\\\\ \\hline ab,\\ ab &amp; abab\\\\ \\hline \\end{array}$$</p><p>Every generated $a$ is immediately followed by $b$.</p><p>Conversely, every string satisfying the condition can be divided into these two types of blocks.</p><p>Thus, <strong>B generates exactly $L$.</strong><br/> </p><p>Now, Lets eliminate the other options</p><p><strong>A :</strong> $(a^*b)^*$</p><p>This expression allows $aab$, because $a^*b$ may contain multiple consecutive occurrences of $a$.</p><p>But $aab$ violates the given condition.</p><p>Therefore, <strong>A is incorrect.</strong></p><p><br/><strong>C :</strong> $a^*b$</p><p>This expression accepts $aab$, which is invalid. It also fails to generate many valid strings.</p><p>Therefore, <strong>C is incorrect.</strong></p><p><br/><strong>D :</strong> $b+(ab)^*$</p><p>This expression allows either the single string $b$ or strings of the form $(ab)^n$.</p><p>It does not generate $bab$, which belongs to $L$.</p><p>Therefore, <strong>D is incorrect.</strong><br/> </p><p><strong>E :</strong> $(ab)^*$</p><p>This expression allows only repetitions of $ab$.</p><p>It cannot generate $b$, $bb$, or $bab$.</p><p>Therefore, <strong>E is incorrect.</strong></p><p><br/>The required regular expression is $\\boxed{(b+ab)^*}$</p><p><strong>Answer : B</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/556246/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-regular-expression#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "NAT",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\">Consider the following statement:<br/> <br/> \"Every subset of a regular language must be regular.\"<br/> <br/> Enter $1$ for True and $0$ for False.</div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [],
            "answer": "0",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"555906\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let, $\\Sigma=\\{a,b\\}$</p><p>Consider, $R=\\Sigma^*$</p><p>This is regular because a DFA with one accepting state and self-loops on $a$ and $b$ recognizes it.</p><p>Define,</p><p>$L=\\{a^nb^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$</p><p>Every string in $L$ is a string over $\\Sigma$.</p><p>$\\therefore L\\subseteq\\Sigma^*$</p><p>The language, $L=\\{a^nb^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$ is not regular.</p><p>It requires an unbounded equality check between the numbers of $a$'s and $b$'s.</p><p>This can be proved using the pumping lemma.</p><p>Suppose $L$ is regular, with pumping length $p$.</p><p>Choose, $w=a^pb^p$</p><p>Every allowed decomposition,</p><p>$w=xyz,\\qquad |xy|\\leq p,\\quad |y|&gt;0$</p><p>must have,</p><p>$y=a^k,\\qquad k\\geq 1$</p><p>Pumping $y$ zero times produces,</p><p>$xy^0z=a^{p-k}b^p$</p><p>This string has unequal numbers of $a$'s and $b$'s.</p><p>$\\therefore xy^0z\\notin L$</p><p>Contradiction.</p><p>So,</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\notin REG}$</p><p>Yet,</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\subseteq R,\\qquad R\\in REG}$</p><p>Hence the statement is false.</p><p><br/><strong>Answer : $0$ (False)</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/555905/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-subset-of-language#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "NAT",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\">Consider the following statement:<br/> <br/> \"Every nonregular language can be expressed as the intersection of countably infinitely many regular languages.\"<br/> <br/> Enter $1$ for True and $0$ for False.</div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [],
            "answer": "1",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"555883\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let $L$ be any nonregular language over an alphabet $\\Sigma$. Its complement, $L^c$, is the set of all strings in $\\Sigma^*$ that are not in $L$.</p><p>$$L^c=\\Sigma^*- L$$</p><p>For any individual string $w\\in L^c$, the language consisting of exactly that one string, $\\{w\\}$, is finite and therefore regular.</p><p>Because regular languages are closed under complementation, the language containing everything <em>except</em> that single string, which is $\\Sigma^*-\\{w\\}$, must also be a regular language.</p><p>By applying De Morgan's Laws, </p><p>We can express the original language $L$ as the intersection of the complements of all the individual strings that are not in $L$:</p><p>$$L=\\bigcap_{w\\in L^c}\\left(\\Sigma^*-\\{w\\}\\right)$$</p><p>The set of all possible strings $\\Sigma^*$ is countably infinite, assuming $\\Sigma$ is a finite, nonempty alphabet.</p><p>Because $L$ is nonregular, its complement $L^c$ cannot be finite.</p><p>If $L^c$ were finite, it would be regular. </p><p>Since regular languages are closed under complementation, this would imply that $L$ is regular, contradicting our assumption.</p><p>Therefore, $L^c$ must contain a countably infinite number of strings.</p><p>Now,</p><p>Because we are taking the intersection of a countably infinite number of regular languages (one for each string in $L^c$), every nonregular language can indeed be expressed as the intersection of countably infinitely many regular languages.</p><p>So, the statement is True.</p><p><strong>Answer : $1$ (True).</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/555882/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-countability#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 2,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "MSQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Consider the following statements about regular and nonregular languages. Which of the statement(s) is/are correct?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The union of a regular language and a nonregular language is always regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The union of a regular language and a nonregular language is always nonregular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The union of two nonregular languages is always nonregular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The complement of a nonregular language is nonregular.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": [
                "A"
            ],
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"555876\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>A : False</strong></p><p>The statement claims $R\\in REG, N\\notin REG$ implies $R\\cup N\\in REG$ for every such pair.</p><p>Choose, $R=\\varnothing$ and $N=\\{a^nb^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$</p><p>We know $R$ is regular and $N$ is nonregular.</p><p>But,</p><p>$R\\cup N=\\varnothing\\cup N=N$ which is nonregular.</p><p>Therefore, <strong>Statement A is false.</strong></p><p><br/><strong>B : False</strong></p><p>Now the claim is that the same kind of union must always be nonregular.</p><p>Choose $R=\\Sigma^*$ where $\\Sigma=\\{a,b\\}$, and let $N=\\{a^nb^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$</p><p>Here $R$ is regular and $N$ is nonregular.</p><p>But, $R\\cup N=\\Sigma^*$ because $N\\subseteq\\Sigma^*$.</p><p>Since $\\Sigma^*$ is regular, the union is regular.</p><p>Therefore, <strong>Statement B is false.</strong><br/> </p><p><strong>Important observation:</strong> A regular language unioned with a nonregular language can produce either a regular or a nonregular language.<br/> </p><p><strong>C : False</strong></p><p>We need a counterexample involving two nonregular languages whose union is regular.</p><p>Let, $L=\\{a^nb^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$ over $\\Sigma=\\{a,b\\}$.</p><p>Define, $L_1=L$ and $L_2=\\Sigma^*\\setminus L$</p><p>Since $L$ is nonregular, its complement is also nonregular.</p><p>Why? If $\\overline L$ were regular, complementing it again would make $L$ regular, contradicting our assumption.</p><p>Thus,</p><p>$L_1\\notin REG,\\qquad L_2\\notin REG$</p><p>However,</p><p>$L_1\\cup L_2=L\\cup\\overline L=\\Sigma^*$</p><p>And $\\Sigma^*$ is regular.</p><p>Therefore, <strong>Statement C is false.</strong></p><p><br/><strong>D : True</strong></p><p>Suppose $L$ is nonregular.</p><p>We want to establish $\\overline L\\notin REG$</p><p>Assume, for contradiction, that $\\overline L\\in REG$</p><p>Regular languages are closed under complementation.</p><p>$\\therefore \\overline{\\overline L}=L\\in REG$</p><p>But this contradicts the assumption that $L$ is nonregular.</p><p>Hence,</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\notin REG\\implies\\overline L\\notin REG}$</p><p><strong>Statement D is true.</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/555875/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-closure-properties#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "NAT",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\">Consider the following statement :<br/> <br/> \"The union of a countable number of regular languages must be regular.\"<br/> <br/> Enter $1$ for True and $0$ for False.</div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [],
            "answer": "0",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"555398\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>We know that regular languages are closed under finite union.</p><p>If $L_1,L_2$ are regular, then $L_1\\cup L_2\\in REG$</p><p>But does the same result hold for a countably infinite union?</p><p>We must distinguish finite union from infinite union.</p><p><br/>For every integer $n\\geq0$, define $L_n=\\{a^nb^n\\}$</p><p>Each $L_n$ contains exactly one string.</p><p>For eg,</p><p>$\\begin{aligned}L_0&amp;=\\{\\epsilon\\}\\\\L_1&amp;=\\{ab\\}\\\\L_2&amp;=\\{aabb\\}\\\\L_3&amp;=\\{aaabbb\\}\\end{aligned}$</p><p>Every singleton language is finite, and every finite language is regular.</p><p>$\\boxed{\\therefore L_n\\in REG\\quad\\forall n\\geq0}$</p><p><br/>Take their infinite union</p><p>Now consider, $L=\\bigcup_{n=0}^{\\infty}L_n$</p><p>This gives, $L=\\{\\epsilon,ab,aabb,aaabbb,\\ldots\\}$</p><p>Equivalently,</p><p>$\\boxed{L=\\{a^nb^n\\mid n\\geq0\\}}$</p><p>But this language is not regular.</p><p>We can prove that using the pumping lemma.</p><p>Suppose $L$ were regular, with pumping length $p$.</p><p>Choose, $w=a^pb^p$</p><p>The pumping lemma requires a decomposition $w=xyz$ satisfying $|xy|\\leq p,\\qquad |y|&gt;0$</p><p>Since the first $p$ symbols are all $a$'s, $y$ contains only $a$'s.</p><p>Write,</p><p>$y=a^k,\\qquad k\\geq1$</p><p>Pump $y$ zero times,</p><p>$xy^0z=a^{p-k}b^p$</p><p>The numbers of $a$'s and $b$'s are unequal.</p><p>$\\therefore xy^0z\\notin L$</p><p>This contradicts the pumping lemma.</p><p>Thus,</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\notin REG}$</p><p><br/>We constructed countably many regular languages whose union is nonregular.</p><p>Therefore, regular languages are not closed under countably infinite union.</p><p><strong>Answer : $\\mathbf{0}$ (False)</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/555397/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-countability#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Which of the following is true?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">There is no known algorithm for checking whether a regular language is nonempty.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The proof of the pumping lemma was a proof by induction.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The language generated by the CFG$$\\begin{array}{l}<br/>A \\rightarrow 0A1 \\\\<br/>A \\rightarrow B \\\\<br/>B \\rightarrow \\epsilon<br/>\\end{array}<br/>$$is nonregular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">None of the above.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"551957\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p><strong>A is false.</strong></p><p>For a regular language represented by a DFA, non-emptiness is decidable.</p><p>Starting at the initial state, simply determine whether any accepting state is reachable.</p><p>If an accepting state is reachable, $L\\neq\\varnothing$.</p><p>If none is reachable, $L=\\varnothing$.</p><p>So there is a known algorithm.</p><p> </p><p><strong>B is false.</strong> </p><p>The usual pumping-lemma argument relies on repetition of states, using the pigeonhole principle.</p><p><br/>For <strong>C</strong>, eliminate $B$:</p><p>$A\\rightarrow 0A1\\mid\\epsilon$.</p><p>$\\therefore L=\\{0^n1^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$.</p><p>This language is not regular.</p><p>Hence,</p><p><strong>Answer : </strong>$\\boxed{\\mathrm{C}}$</p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/551956/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-regular-language-non-emptiness#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Consider the following regular expressions over the alphabet</p><p>$\\Sigma=\\{a,b\\}$.</p><p>$R_1=a(a\\cup b)^*$</p><p>$R_2=b(a\\cup b)^*$</p><p>If $L(R)$ denotes the language associated with regular expression $R$, which statement is correct?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L(R_1)=L(R_2)$<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L(R_2)=\\{w\\mid w\\ \\mathrm{ends\\ with}\\ b\\}$<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">There exists a deterministic finite automaton whose language is $L(R_1)\\cup L(R_2)$.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">If $R_3$ is a regular expression such that $L(R_3)=L(R_1)\\cap L(R_2)$, then $L(R_3)$ is infinite.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">An NFA recognizing $L(R_1)\\cup L(R_2)$ must have at least four states.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"551944\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>First,</p><p>$L(R_1)$ contains all nonempty strings beginning with $a$, while</p><p>$L(R_2)$ contains all nonempty strings beginning with $b$.</p><p>$\\therefore L(R_1)\\cup L(R_2)=\\{a,b\\}^+$</p><p>This is a regular language, so there certainly exists a DFA recognizing it.</p><p>Hence <strong>C</strong> is true.</p><p><strong>A</strong> is false because one language starts with $a$, while the other starts with $b$.</p><p><strong>B</strong> is false because $R_2$ specifies strings <strong>starting</strong> with $b$, not ending with $b$.</p><p>Also,</p><p>$L(R_1)\\cap L(R_2)=\\varnothing$.</p><p>So <strong>D</strong> is false.</p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/551943/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-regular-expression#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Let \\(I \\subseteq \\Sigma^*\\) be any nonregular language.</p><p>For every string \\(w \\notin I\\), define $R_w = \\Sigma^* - \\{w\\}.$</p><p>Now consider $K = \\bigcap_{w \\notin I} R_w.$</p><p>What is $K$?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">\\(K = \\Sigma^*\\)<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">\\(K = \\overline{I}\\)<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">\\(K = I\\)<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">\\(K = \\varnothing\\)</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550463\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>For every $w\\notin I$,</p><p>$R_w=\\Sigma^*-\\{w\\}$.</p><p>Since the singleton language $\\{w\\}$ is finite, it is regular.</p><p>Regular languages are closed under complement, so every $R_w$ is regular.</p><p>Now consider</p><p>$K=\\bigcap_{w\\notin I}(\\Sigma^*-\\{w\\})$.</p><p>Every string outside $I$ is removed by its corresponding language $R_w$.</p><p>Strings belonging to $I$ are never removed.</p><p>Hence exactly the strings of $I$ remain.</p><p>$\\therefore K=I$.</p><p>Since $I$ is chosen to be nonregular, $K$ is nonregular even though every language in the intersection is regular.</p><p>Therefore, regular languages are not closed under arbitrary infinite intersection.</p><p><strong>Answer : C</strong></p><p> </p><p><em><strong>Note : </strong></em><br/>Regular languages are closed under $\\boxed{\\text{finite intersection}}$ but not necessarily under arbitrary infinite intersection.</p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550462/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-infinite-intersection#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"6773\" data-start=\"6719\" dir=\"auto\">Suppose $L_1$ and $L_2$ are both nonregular languages.</p><p data-end=\"6796\" data-start=\"6775\" dir=\"auto\">Which statement about $L_1\\cup L_2$ is correct?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It must be nonregular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It must be regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It may be regular or nonregular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It must be context-free.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550256\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>We need to show that <strong>both possibilities can occur</strong>.</p><p><strong>Case $\\mathbf1:$ The union can be nonregular</strong></p><p>Let</p><p>$L_1=L_2=\\{0^n1^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$.</p><p>Both languages are nonregular.</p><p>Then</p><p>$L_1\\cup L_2=\\{0^n1^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$,</p><p>which is still nonregular.</p><p>So the union <strong>can be nonregular</strong>.<br/> </p><p><strong>Case $\\mathbf2:$ The union can be regular</strong></p><p>Take any nonregular language $L$.</p><p>Its complement $\\overline{L}$ must also be nonregular.</p><p data-end=\"449\" data-start=\"413\" dir=\"auto\">Suppose $\\overline{L}$ were regular.</p><p data-end=\"503\" data-start=\"451\" dir=\"auto\">Since regular languages are closed under complement,</p><p data-end=\"532\" data-start=\"505\" dir=\"auto\">$\\overline{\\overline{L}}=L$</p><p data-end=\"582\" data-start=\"534\" dir=\"auto\">would also be regular, which is a contradiction.</p><p data-end=\"594\" data-start=\"584\" dir=\"auto\">Now choose</p><p data-end=\"628\" data-start=\"596\" dir=\"auto\">$L_1=L,\\qquad L_2=\\overline{L}$.</p><p data-end=\"670\" data-start=\"630\" dir=\"auto\">Both $L_1$ and $L_2$ are nonregular, but</p><p data-end=\"713\" data-start=\"672\" dir=\"auto\">$L_1\\cup L_2=L\\cup\\overline{L}=\\Sigma^*$, which is regular.</p><p>Therefore, the union of two nonregular languages can be either regular or nonregular.</p><p>So the class of nonregular languages is <strong>not closed under union</strong>.<br/> </p><p><strong>Answer : C</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550255/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-nonregular-union#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"5437\" data-start=\"5404\" dir=\"auto\">Let $L$ be a nonregular language.</p><p data-end=\"5480\" data-start=\"5439\" dir=\"auto\">What can always be concluded about $L^R$?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ is regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ is nonregular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ may be regular or nonregular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Nothing can be concluded unless $L$ is context-free.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550254\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"434\" data-start=\"402\" dir=\"auto\">Given that $L$ is nonregular.</p><p data-end=\"434\" data-start=\"402\" dir=\"auto\">Suppose, for contradiction, that $L^R$ is regular.</p><p data-end=\"500\" data-start=\"456\" dir=\"auto\">Regular languages are closed under reversal.</p><p data-end=\"512\" data-start=\"502\" dir=\"auto\">Therefore,</p><p data-end=\"523\" data-start=\"514\" dir=\"auto\">$(L^R)^R\\in \\text{REG}$.</p><p data-end=\"551\" data-start=\"548\" dir=\"auto\">But $(L^R)^R=L$.</p><p data-end=\"662\" data-start=\"567\" dir=\"auto\">This implies that $L$ is regular, which contradicts the given condition that $L$ is nonregular.</p><p data-end=\"670\" data-start=\"664\" dir=\"auto\">Hence,</p><p data-end=\"727\" data-start=\"672\" dir=\"auto\">$L\\text{ nonregular}\\Rightarrow L^R\\text{ nonregular}$.</p><p data-end=\"739\" data-start=\"729\" dir=\"auto\"><strong>Answer : B</strong></p><p data-end=\"739\" data-start=\"729\" dir=\"auto\"> </p><p data-end=\"739\" data-start=\"729\" dir=\"auto\"><em><strong>Note : </strong></em></p><p data-end=\"739\" data-start=\"729\" dir=\"auto\">Closure of <strong data-end=\"6230\" data-start=\"6209\">regular languages</strong> under reversal automatically gives us a corresponding fact about nonregular languages:</p><p data-end=\"739\" data-start=\"729\" dir=\"auto\">$\\boxed{\\text{Nonregular languages are closed under reversal.}}$</p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550253/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-nonregular-reversal#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Regular Expression & Regular Languages</a></b></div></div>"
        }
    ]
});

