registerTest({
    "name": "DPP 23 - Theory of Computation (Pushdown Automata & CFL - Part 1)",
    "testLabel": "DPP 23",
    "testType": "DPP",
    "bracket": "Pushdown Automata & CFL - Part 1",
    "subject": "toc",
    "series": "cse-dpp-2027",
    "date": "Nov 03, 2026",
    "duration": "30 Min",
    "totalMarks": 13,
    "topicsCovered": "Pushdown Automata & CFL (Practice Set 1)",
    "questions": [
        {
            "marks": 2,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "MSQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Consider the language, $L=\\{a^nb^{2n}c\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$ and the grammar $G=(\\{S,X\\},\\{a,b,c\\},P,S)$ with productions:</p><p>$$\\begin{aligned} S\\rightarrow &amp;Xc\\\\X\\rightarrow &amp;aXbb\\mid\\epsilon\\end{aligned}$$</p><p>Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L$ is a context-free language.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Grammar $G$ generates the language $L$.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L$ cannot be recognized by a pushdown automaton.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">None of the above.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": [
                "A"
            ],
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"556254\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>We need to determine what the grammar generates and whether a pushdown automaton (PDA) can recognize the language.</p><p><strong>A : True</strong></p><p>Consider, $L=\\{a^nb^{2n}c\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$</p><p>The strings in $L$ contain:</p><ul><li>$n$ occurrences of $a$.</li><li>Exactly $2n$ occurrences of $b$.</li><li>One final $c$.</li></ul><p>For eg,</p><p>$$\\begin{array}{|c|c|}\\hline n &amp; \\text{String}\\\\ \\hline 0 &amp; c\\\\ \\hline 1 &amp; abbc\\\\ \\hline 2 &amp; aabbbbc\\\\ \\hline 3 &amp; aaabbbbbbc\\\\ \\hline \\end{array}$$</p><p>A pushdown automaton can check this relationship.</p><p>For each $a$ read, the PDA can push two stack markers.</p><p>For each $b$, it pops one marker.</p><p>After all markers have been removed, it checks for the final $c$.</p><p>Thus, the language is context-free.</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\in\\mathrm{CFL}}$</p><p>Hence, <strong>Statement A is true.</strong></p><p><br/><strong>B : True</strong></p><p>Now examine the grammar:</p><p>$S\\rightarrow Xc$</p><p>$X\\rightarrow aXbb\\mid\\epsilon$</p><p>The recursive production $X\\rightarrow aXbb$ adds one $a$ at the beginning and two $b$'s at the end.</p><p>Let's derive some strings.</p><p><strong>For $n=0$:</strong></p><p>$S\\Rightarrow Xc\\Rightarrow c$</p><p><strong>For $n=1$:</strong></p><p>$S\\Rightarrow Xc\\Rightarrow aXbbc\\Rightarrow abbc$</p><p><strong>For $n=2$:</strong></p><p>$\\begin{aligned} S&amp;\\Rightarrow Xc\\\\ &amp;\\Rightarrow aXbbc\\\\ &amp;\\Rightarrow aaXbbbbc\\\\ &amp;\\Rightarrow aabbbbc \\end{aligned}$</p><p>After $n$ recursive applications:</p><p>$X\\Rightarrow^*a^nXb^{2n}$</p><p>Finally, using $X\\rightarrow\\epsilon$:</p><p>$S\\Rightarrow^*a^nb^{2n}c$</p><p>$\\boxed{\\therefore L(G)=L}$</p><p>Hence, <strong>Statement B is true.</strong></p><p><br/><strong>C : False</strong></p><p>Every context-free language can be recognized by a nondeterministic pushdown automaton.</p><p>Since, $L\\in\\mathrm{CFL}$ there exists a PDA recognizing $L$.</p><p>Therefore, <strong>Statement C is false.</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/556252/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-cfg#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 2,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "MSQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Consider the languages:</p><p>$$L=\\{0^n1^n2^i\\mid n\\geq 0,\\ i\\geq 0\\} \\quad \\text{and}\\quad M=\\{0^i1^n2^n\\mid n\\geq 0,\\ i\\geq 0\\}$$</p><p>Which statement is correct?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The language $L\\cup M$ can be generated by a context-free grammar.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The language $M$ can be generated by a regular grammar.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The language $L$ can be accepted by a deterministic finite automaton.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The language $L\\cap M$ is context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The language $M$ can be represented by a regular expression.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": [
                "A"
            ],
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"555920\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>We are given two languages.</p><p>$L=\\{0^n1^n2^i\\mid n,i\\geq 0\\}$, contains strings where the numbers of $0$'s and $1$'s are equal, followed by any number of $2$'s.</p><p>eg, $\\epsilon, 01, 0011, 012, 001122$.</p><p>Similarly,</p><p>$M=\\{0^i1^n2^n\\mid n,i\\geq 0\\}$, contains strings with any number of $0$'s, followed by equal numbers of $1$'s and $2$'s.</p><p>eg, $\\epsilon,12, 012, 0012, 1122$</p><p>Now,</p><p>We can express $L$ as, $L=\\{0^n1^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}\\{2^i\\mid i\\geq 0\\}$</p><p>The language $\\{0^n1^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$ is context-free.</p><p>The language $2^*$ is regular and therefore context-free.</p><p>Context-free languages are closed under concatenation.</p><p>Hence,</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\in CFL}$</p><p>We can also give an explicit grammar:</p><p>$S\\rightarrow AB$</p><p>$A\\rightarrow 0A1\\mid\\epsilon$</p><p>$B\\rightarrow 2B\\mid\\epsilon$</p><p>Now Similarly,</p><p>$M=0^*\\{1^n2^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$</p><p>A grammar for $M$ is,</p><p>$S\\rightarrow AB$</p><p>$A\\rightarrow 0A\\mid\\epsilon$</p><p>$B\\rightarrow 1B2\\mid\\epsilon$</p><p>Thus, $\\boxed{M\\in CFL}$</p><p><br/><strong>A : True</strong></p><p>The question claims that $L\\cup M$ can be generated by a context-free grammar.</p><p>We already proved,</p><p>$L\\in CFL,\\qquad M\\in CFL$</p><p>Context-free languages are closed under union.</p><p>$\\boxed{\\therefore L\\cup M\\in CFL}$</p><p>We can even construct a grammar:</p><p>$S\\rightarrow S_L\\mid S_M$</p><p>where $S_L$ and $S_M$ generate $L$ and $M$, respectively.</p><p>So A is true.</p><p><br/><strong>B : False</strong></p><p>A regular grammar generates only regular languages.</p><p>But, $M=0^*\\{1^n2^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$ is not regular.</p><p>To see why, suppose $M$ were regular.</p><p>Intersect $M$ with the regular language, $R=1^*2^*$</p><p>The result is,</p><p>$M\\cap R=\\{1^n2^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$</p><p>which is nonregular.</p><p>However, regular languages are closed under intersection.</p><p>Contradiction.</p><p>Therefore $M$ is not regular and cannot be generated by a regular grammar.</p><p><br/><strong>C : False</strong></p><p>The language $L$ contains the nonregular dependency, $0^n1^n$</p><p>If $L$ were regular, intersecting it with the regular language $0^*1^*$ would produce $\\{0^n1^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}$ which is not regular.</p><p>Hence,</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\notin REG}$</p><p>A DFA recognizes only regular languages.</p><p>Therefore C is false.</p><p><br/><strong>D : False</strong></p><p>This is the central trap.</p><p>We need to determine, $L\\cap M$</p><p>For a string, $0^a1^b2^c$ to belong to $L$, we require $a=b$</p><p>For the same string to belong to $M$, we require $b=c$</p><p>Both conditions together imply, $a=b=c$</p><p>$\\boxed{\\therefore L\\cap M=\\{0^n1^n2^n\\mid n\\geq 0\\}}$</p><p>But this is a standard non-context-free language.</p><p>It requires simultaneous equality of three symbol counts.</p><p>A single-stack pushdown automaton cannot recognize this language, and its non-context-freeness can be established using the pumping lemma for CFLs.</p><p>Hence,</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\cap M\\notin CFL}$</p><p>Thus D is false.</p><p><br/><strong>E : False</strong></p><p>Regular expressions describe exactly the regular languages.</p><p>Since $M$ is nonregular, no regular expression can describe it.</p><p>Therefore E is false.</p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/555912/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-cfl-closure-properties#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>Which one of the following statements concerning pushdown automata is <strong>false</strong>?</p><p>Here, \"accept\" means acceptance by empty stack.</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Every context-free language is accepted by some deterministic PDA.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">The language accepted by any non-deterministic PDA is a context-free language.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">For any non-deterministic PDA, there is a non-deterministic PDA with just one state that accepts the same language.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">Every $\\mathrm{LL(1)}$ parse table gives rise to a deterministic PDA.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">There are context-sensitive languages that are not accepted by any non-deterministic PDA.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"552518\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>The crucial distinction is $\\mathrm{DCFL}\\subsetneq\\mathrm{CFL}$</p><p>A deterministic PDA recognizes only <strong>deterministic context-free languages</strong>, which form a proper subclass of all context-free languages.</p><p>Therefore, there are CFLs that require nondeterminism and cannot be recognized by any DPDA.</p><p>So statement <strong>A is false</strong>.</p><p>The remaining statements are consistent with standard PDA results:</p><p>An NPDA recognizes a context-free language.</p><p>NPDA control states can be encoded into stack information, allowing an equivalent one-state construction under the stated acceptance convention.</p><p>$LL(1)$ parsing is deterministic, so it can be implemented using a DPDA.</p><p>NPDAs recognize exactly the CFLs, while context-sensitive languages include languages outside the CFL class.</p><p>Hence,</p><p><strong>Answer : </strong>$\\boxed{\\mathrm{A}}$</p><p><br/><strong><em>Note :</em></strong></p><p>$\\boxed{\\mathrm{DPDA\\ power}&lt;\\mathrm{NPDA\\ power}}$</p><p>This is different from finite automata, where DFA and NFA have equal expressive power.</p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/552517/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-dpda-vs-npda#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"4011\" data-start=\"3958\" dir=\"auto\">Suppose $L$ is a deterministic context-free language.</p><p data-end=\"4059\" data-start=\"4013\" dir=\"auto\">Which of the following is always correct about</p><p>$$L^R=\\{w^R\\mid w\\in L\\}?$$</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ must be regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ must be deterministic context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ must be context-free, but need not be deterministic context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ need not even be context-free.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550248\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"128\" data-start=\"97\" dir=\"auto\">Since every DCFL is a CFL,</p><p data-end=\"128\" data-start=\"97\" dir=\"auto\">$L\\in \\text{DCFL} \\Rightarrow L\\in \\text{CFL}$</p><p data-end=\"171\" data-start=\"130\" dir=\"auto\">CFLs are closed under reversal.</p><p data-end=\"171\" data-start=\"130\" dir=\"auto\">$\\therefore L^R\\in \\text{CFL}$.</p><p data-end=\"233\" data-start=\"188\" dir=\"auto\">So options A and D are immediately ruled out.</p><p data-end=\"233\" data-start=\"188\" dir=\"auto\">The key point is that DCFLs are not closed under reversal. </p><p data-end=\"233\" data-start=\"188\" dir=\"auto\">$\\therefore L\\in \\text{DCFL} \\nRightarrow L^R\\in \\text{DCFL}$.</p><p data-end=\"5056\" data-start=\"5051\" dir=\"auto\">while, $L\\in \\text{DCFL} \\Rightarrow L^R\\in \\text{CFL}.$</p><p data-end=\"5109\" data-start=\"5099\" dir=\"auto\">Hence $L^R$ is guaranteed to be context-free, but it need not remain deterministic context-free.</p><p data-end=\"473\" data-start=\"449\" dir=\"auto\"><strong>Answer : C</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550245/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-dcfl-reversal#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"2413\" data-start=\"2405\" dir=\"auto\">Consider $$L= \\{a^ib^jc^k\\mid i=j\\text{ or }j=k\\}.$$ Which of the following correctly classifies $L$?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L$ is regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L$ is DCFL but not regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L$ is CFL but not DCFL.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L$ is not context-free.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550244\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p>First write</p><p>$L=L_1\\cup L_2$</p><p>where</p><p>$L_1=\\{a^ib^ic^k\\mid i,k\\geq 0\\}$</p><p>and $L_2=\\{a^ib^jc^j\\mid i,j\\geq 0\\}$.</p><p>Both $L_1$ and $L_2$ are context-free languages.</p><p>Since CFLs are closed under union,</p><p>$L\\in CFL$.</p><p>Now suppose, for contradiction, that</p><p>$L\\in DCFL$.</p><p>DCFLs are closed under complement. Therefore,</p><p>$\\overline{L}\\in DCFL$.</p><p>Since every DCFL is a CFL,</p><p>$\\overline{L}\\in CFL$.</p><p>Now intersect $\\overline{L}$ with the regular language $R=a^*b^*c^*$.</p><p>Then,</p><p>$\\overline{L}\\cap R = \\{a^ib^jc^k\\mid i\\neq j\\text{ and }j\\neq k\\}$.</p><p>But</p><p>$\\{a^ib^jc^k\\mid i\\neq j\\text{ and }j\\neq k\\}$ is known to be <strong>not context-free</strong>.</p><p>However, CFLs are closed under intersection with regular languages.</p><p>Thus, if $\\overline{L}$ were context-free, then $\\overline{L}\\cap R$ would also have to be context-free.</p><p>This is a contradiction.</p><p>Therefore, $L\\notin DCFL$.</p><p>We already know that $L\\in CFL$.</p><p>Hence,</p><p>$\\boxed{L\\in CFL-DCFL}$.</p><p><strong>Answer : C</strong></p><p> </p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550241/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-dcfl-classification#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 2,
            "neg": 0,
            "type": "MSQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"1032\" data-start=\"944\" dir=\"auto\">Let $L_1,L_2$ be deterministic context-free languages and let $R$ be a regular language.</p><p data-end=\"1088\" data-start=\"1034\" dir=\"auto\">Which of the following statements are <strong data-end=\"1087\" data-start=\"1072\">always true</strong>?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L_1\\cup L_2$ is deterministic context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$\\overline{L_1}$ is deterministic context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L_1\\cap R$ is deterministic context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L_1\\cap L_2$ is deterministic context-free.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": [
                "A"
            ],
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550240\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"1401\" data-start=\"1380\" dir=\"auto\"><strong data-end=\"1415\" data-start=\"1403\">A. False</strong></p><p data-end=\"1537\" data-start=\"1417\" dir=\"auto\">DCFLs are <strong data-end=\"1453\" data-start=\"1427\">not closed under union</strong>. </p><p data-end=\"1544\" data-start=\"1539\" dir=\"auto\">Thus, $L_1,L_2\\in \\text{DCFL}$ does not guarantee</p><p>$L_1\\cup L_2\\in \\text{DCFL}.$<br/> </p><p data-end=\"1628\" data-start=\"1617\" dir=\"auto\"><strong data-end=\"1628\" data-start=\"1617\">B. True</strong></p><p data-end=\"1664\" data-start=\"1630\" dir=\"auto\">DCFLs are closed under complement:</p><p>$L\\in \\text{DCFL} \\Rightarrow \\overline L\\in \\text{DCFL}.$</p><p data-end=\"1780\" data-start=\"1716\" dir=\"auto\">This is one of the important differences between CFLs and DCFLs.<br/> </p><p data-end=\"1793\" data-start=\"1782\" dir=\"auto\"><strong data-end=\"1793\" data-start=\"1782\">C. True</strong></p><p data-end=\"1855\" data-start=\"1795\" dir=\"auto\">DCFLs are closed under intersection with a regular language:</p><p>$L\\in \\text{DCFL},\\quad R\\in \\text{REG}$</p><p data-end=\"1896\" data-start=\"1889\" dir=\"auto\">implies</p><p>$L\\cap R\\in \\text{DCFL}$.<br/> </p><p data-end=\"2007\" data-start=\"1922\" dir=\"auto\"><strong data-end=\"2021\" data-start=\"2009\">D. False</strong></p><p data-end=\"2063\" data-start=\"2023\" dir=\"auto\">DCFLs are not closed under intersection.</p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550239/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-dcfl-closure#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"4478\" data-start=\"4452\" dir=\"auto\">For a language $L$, define</p><p>$$L^R=\\{w^R\\mid w\\in L\\}.$$ If $L$ is context-free, which statement is correct?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ is always regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ is always context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ may fail to be context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^R$ is context-free only if $L$ is deterministic context-free.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550082\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"4833\" data-start=\"4801\" dir=\"auto\">Let $G$ be a CFG generating $L$.</p><p data-end=\"4855\" data-start=\"4835\" dir=\"auto\">For every production</p><p>$A\\rightarrow X_1X_2\\cdots X_k,$</p><p data-end=\"4931\" data-start=\"4895\" dir=\"auto\">construct a corresponding production</p><p>$A\\rightarrow X_kX_{k-1}\\cdots X_1.$</p><p data-end=\"5039\" data-start=\"4975\" dir=\"auto\">In other words, reverse the right-hand side of every production.</p><p data-end=\"5072\" data-start=\"5041\" dir=\"auto\">If the original grammar derives $w,$ the transformed grammar derives $w^R.$</p><p data-end=\"5159\" data-start=\"5129\" dir=\"auto\">Therefore it generates exactly $L^R.$</p><p data-end=\"5225\" data-start=\"5173\" dir=\"auto\">Since the transformed grammar is still context-free, $L^R \\in CFL.$</p><p data-end=\"5225\" data-start=\"5173\" dir=\"auto\"><strong>Answer : B</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550081/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-cfl-reversal-closure#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"3268\" data-start=\"3206\" dir=\"auto\">Suppose a language $L$ is accepted by some pushdown automaton.</p><p data-end=\"3311\" data-start=\"3270\" dir=\"auto\">What can always be concluded about $L^*$?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^*$ must be regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^*$ must be context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^*$ need not be context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$L^*$ is context-free only when $L$ is finite.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "B",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550079\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"3551\" data-start=\"3525\" dir=\"auto\">If a PDA accepts $L$, then</p><p>$L\\in CFL.$</p><p data-end=\"3622\" data-start=\"3570\" dir=\"auto\">Context-free languages are closed under Kleene star.</p><p data-end=\"3634\" data-start=\"3624\" dir=\"auto\">Therefore,</p><p>$L\\in CFL \\implies L^*\\in CFL.$</p><p data-end=\"3705\" data-start=\"3673\" dir=\"auto\">We can also see this using CFGs.</p><p data-end=\"3760\" data-start=\"3707\" dir=\"auto\">Suppose $G$ is a CFG for $L$ with start variable $S$.</p><p data-end=\"3829\" data-start=\"3762\" dir=\"auto\">Introduce a new start variable $S_0$ and use a construction such as</p><p>$S_0\\rightarrow SS_0\\mid\\epsilon.$</p><p data-end=\"3943\" data-start=\"3871\" dir=\"auto\">This permits zero or more strings generated from $L$ to be concatenated.</p><p data-end=\"3982\" data-start=\"3945\" dir=\"auto\">Hence the resulting grammar generates $L^*$.</p><p data-end=\"4121\" data-start=\"3996\" dir=\"auto\">Notice that $L^*$ does <strong data-end=\"4026\" data-start=\"4019\">not</strong> have to be regular. Closure under star preserves context-freeness, not necessarily regularity.</p><p data-end=\"4121\" data-start=\"3996\" dir=\"auto\"><strong>Answer : B</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550078/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-kleene-star-closure#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"2184\" data-start=\"2096\" dir=\"auto\">Let $R$ be a regular language and $C$ be a context-free language over the same alphabet.</p><p data-end=\"2207\" data-start=\"2186\" dir=\"auto\">Which statement about $R\\cup C$ is always true?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$R\\cup C$ must be regular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$R\\cup C$ must be context-free.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$R\\cup C$ must be nonregular.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">$R\\cup C$ may fail to be context-free.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "A",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550077\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"2506\" data-start=\"2451\" dir=\"auto\">Every regular language is also a context-free language.</p><p data-end=\"2518\" data-start=\"2508\" dir=\"auto\">Therefore,</p><p>$R\\in REG \\implies R\\in CFL$</p><p data-end=\"2567\" data-start=\"2555\" dir=\"auto\">We are given</p><p>$C\\in CFL.$</p><p data-end=\"2620\" data-start=\"2586\" dir=\"auto\">CFLs are closed under union, hence</p><p>$R\\cup C\\in CFL.$</p><p data-end=\"2676\" data-start=\"2645\" dir=\"auto\">But it need <strong data-end=\"2664\" data-start=\"2657\">not</strong> be regular.</p><p data-end=\"2695\" data-start=\"2678\" dir=\"auto\">For example, take</p><p>$R=\\varnothing$ and $C=\\{a^nb^n\\mid n\\ge0\\}$.</p><p data-end=\"2758\" data-start=\"2754\" dir=\"auto\">Then</p><p>$R\\cup C=C,$ which is context-free but nonregular.</p><p data-end=\"2865\" data-start=\"2817\" dir=\"auto\">Therefore the strongest guaranteed conclusion is</p><p>$\\boxed{R\\cup C\\text{ is context-free}}.$</p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550076/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-cfl-regular-union#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        },
        {
            "marks": 1,
            "neg": 0.33,
            "type": "MCQ",
            "text": "<span style=\"display: inline;\"><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"836\" data-start=\"763\" dir=\"auto\">The class of context-free languages satisfies which one of the following?</p></div></span>",
            "image": "",
            "options": [
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It is closed under intersection.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It is closed under complementation.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It is closed under concatenation.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It contains every possible language.<br/></span>",
                "<span style=\"display: inline;\">It does not include every regular language.</span>"
            ],
            "answer": "C",
            "solution": "<div style=\"background-color: #bae1c4; border: 1px solid #75c98a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 10px 15px; margin-top: 15px; color: #155724; font-family: sans-serif;\">  <strong style=\"font-size: 16px; color: #000;\">Explanation:</strong><br> <a name=\"550069\"></a><div itemprop=\"text\"><p data-end=\"1151\" data-start=\"1112\" dir=\"auto\">Context-free languages are closed under</p><p>$\\text{Union},\\quad \\text{Concatenation},\\quad \\text{Kleene Star},\\quad \\text{Reversal}$</p><p data-end=\"1258\" data-start=\"1248\" dir=\"auto\">Therefore,</p><p>$L_1,L_2\\in CFL \\implies L_1L_2\\in CFL$</p><p data-end=\"1323\" data-start=\"1306\" dir=\"auto\">So <strong data-end=\"1322\" data-start=\"1309\">C is true</strong>.</p><p data-end=\"1356\" data-start=\"1325\" dir=\"auto\">Now consider the other options.</p><p data-end=\"1414\" data-start=\"1358\" dir=\"auto\">CFLs are <strong data-end=\"1374\" data-start=\"1367\">not</strong> closed under intersection. For example,</p><p>$L_1=\\{a^nb^nc^m\\mid n,m\\ge0\\}$</p><p data-end=\"1456\" data-start=\"1453\" dir=\"auto\">and</p><p>$L_2=\\{a^mb^nc^n\\mid n,m\\ge0\\}$</p><p data-end=\"1521\" data-start=\"1495\" dir=\"auto\">are both context-free, but</p><p>$L_1\\cap L_2 = \\{a^nb^nc^n\\mid n\\ge0\\},$</p><p data-end=\"1595\" data-start=\"1569\" dir=\"auto\">which is not context-free.</p><p data-end=\"1643\" data-start=\"1597\" dir=\"auto\">CFLs are also <strong data-end=\"1618\" data-start=\"1611\">not</strong> closed under complement.</p><p data-end=\"1706\" data-start=\"1645\" dir=\"auto\">Every regular language is context-free, so option E is false.<br/> </p><p data-end=\"1714\" data-start=\"1708\" dir=\"auto\"><strong>Answer : C</strong></p></div><br><br><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/550067/go-classes-dpp-gate-cs-theory-of-computation-cfl-closure#a_list\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none; color: #2e7bc5; font-size: 15px;\">Click here for detail solution by gateoverflow</a><div class=\"year_sub_chap_link\" style=\"margin-top:5px; font-size:14px;\"><b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/goclasses-toc-practice-questions\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">GO Classes DPP</a></b> <b><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/tag/theory-of-computation\" style=\"color:#2f6d1a; text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\">TOC - Pushdown Automata & CFL</a></b></div></div>"
        }
    ]
});

